{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to AnyDesk for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Did God have any choice in creating the \nworld? So asked Albert Einstein. He was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8560,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being poetic. What he really meant, was whether \nthe universe could have been any other way. Could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it have had different laws of physics, driven by \ndifferent fundamental constants. Or is this one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vast and complex universe the inevitable result \nof an inevitable and unique underlying principle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps expressible as a supremely elegant \nTheory of Everything. It certainly seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Einstein thought this should be the \ncase … that God had no choice in whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or how to create the world. It seems \nlike a pretty arm-chair philosophical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45280,
    "dDurationMs": 11120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps unanswerable question, but the modern \n“problem” of naturalness may lead to an answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everybody. A couple quick announcements before \nwe get started. Our second patreon interview with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66000,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Mithuna Yorganthan is available. We had a \ngreat chat about quantum entanglement, quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computing and various quantum interpretations. \nThere’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next, we’re running our next algorithm experiment \nfor the next couple of episodes. Our algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has shied away from sharing our episodes with the \nfull space time community again, so we’re trying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to determine whether likes or comments are more \ninfluential. For this phase of the experiment we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to see what happens if we can get 60,000+ \nlikes per episode. If you want to be a data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in this experiment, just hit the like button \nand of course feel free to comment as per usual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, we also have our July merch available. \nThis is a special one. Retired scientist Dr. Jim  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Slater is a fan of space time and the creator of \nthis incredible image of how the earth and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun move the earth and the sun move around the \nmilky way galaxy. And he gave us his blessing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to put it on a t-shirt and sweatshirt, so you can \nshow everyone in your local patch of space time a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133040,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientifically accurate movement of the sun and \nearth. We also have two new black hole logo space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138951,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time caps, one with a white hole and one with \nan all black hole. Links in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144760,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Naturalness or fine-tuning problems in general are \nthose in which some property of the universe seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oddly specific—as though whatever process decided \non the parameters of the natural laws cared that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this particular property has the value it does. \nWe’ve been talking about this a bit, including  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in two recent episodes where we discussed why many \nphysicists think that the small value of the mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Higgs boson seems unnatural—this is called \nthe hierarchy problem. The idea, if you recall,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that there should be processes influencing the \nHiggs mass that operate at very high energies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which should in turn lead to a very high Higgs \nmass unless there’s some precision suppression  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those influences. Those influences \nare interactions with quantum fields,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194480,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the suppression is supposed to be that they \ncancel each other out. No “natural” mechanism has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet been discovered to achieve such high precision \nand coordinated cancelling, but the alternative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinated canceling is fortuitous cancelling. If \nthe latter is true then it looks like the various  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancellations were “finely tuned” to achieve \nthat small Higgs mass, which feels unnatural."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another supposed fine-tuning problem \nis the apparently very small value of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmological constant. So we observe that \nthe expansion of the universe is accelerating,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a possible culprit is vacuum energy—a faint \nenergy density in the quantum fields that fill  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of space. We call this influence dark energy \nand describe it with the cosmological constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the Einstein equations. And for basically the \nsame reason that physicists are confused by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low Higgs mass, many are also confused \nthat dark energy isn’t extremely strong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the contributions of high-energy \ncomponents of those fields. And again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum cancellations could reduce this energy. \nIt’s not such a stretch to imagine a perfect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancellation if there’s a high level of \nsymmetry in those contributions. But to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268240,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancel almost but not quite perfectly seems an \noddly specific result. Finely tuned, in fact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far we’ve been talking about these \nissues in very mechanistic terms.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a potential source of confusion, \nbecause in a sense the mechanisms don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter. There’s a much more general way \nto express the problem that gets to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heart of the questions we started with: Is \nour very particular universe inevitable?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m going to get to that after a few more \nwords to put the mechanistic picture to bed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum field theory describes essentially \neverything in terms of myriad interactions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between quantum fields, expressed in this theory \nin terms of virtual particle interactions. Many,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps most physicists understand this virtual \nstuff to be just a mathematical tool representing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the messy interactions of quantum fields. But \neven if the Feynman sums in QFT are mathematical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fictions, we can still consider the complex field \ninteractions they represent as being “real” in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some sense, whatever real really means. The \nastonishing success of the standard model of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340800,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle physics, itself a QFT, means we kind of \nhave to take quantum field interactions seriously.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That includes ideas like field interactions \ncontributing to particle mass and even the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea of field interactions canceling each others’ \ninfluence. There’s also independent evidence that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this “virtual” activity is in a sense real—for \nexample the Casimir effect, in which a measurable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force is induced by excluding supposed virtual \nfield modes from between two conducting plates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the clockwork behind the Standard Model—the \nmechanism of quantum field interactions and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancelations—make very sensible predictions in \nsome cases, and nonsensical ones in other cases.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, prior to being fixed, the QFT \nbehind the standard model predicted the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391440,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass and dark energy should be enormous. \nCan we just ignore those predictions?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the standard model, the answer \nwas yes. At least, if all we want to do is to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404960,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use the theory in its domain of applicability. \nThe supposed exploding masses of the particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be dealt with if we just input by hand \ntheir true masses as measured in the lab.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The process is called renormalization, and \nit involves adding some made-up canceling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms to get the exploding masses back down to \nwhere we know they should be. With this change,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430160,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those particles' masses are no \nlonger predicted by the theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432960,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather become free parameters. In this way the \nStandard Model became internally self-consistent.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t predict very large masses, but \nthat’s by construction. It’s the unconstrained,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "un-renormalized quantum field theory underneath \nthe standard model that predicts large masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During the development of the Standard Model, \nsome physicists had hoped that the quantum field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory on which the model is built would be able \nto fully explain the masses of the particles. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were not pleased that this feature didn’t make it \ninto the release version of the theory. Richard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feynman, who led the development of quantum \nelectrodynamics—the electromagnetic part of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard model—called renormalization \na “shell game” and a “dippy process”.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He said “such hocus-pocus has prevented \nus from proving that the theory of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum electrodynamics is mathematically \nself-consistent”. Paul Dirac said he was,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“very dissatisfied with the situation because \nthis so-called 'good theory' does involve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neglecting infinities which appear in its \nequations, ignoring them in an arbitrary way.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two takeaways here. One is that the \nunderlying mechanics of the Standard Model does  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead to confusing stuff about the particles masses \nif you remove the hocus-pocus of renormalization.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And some would argue that sans-hocus-pocus, \nif you take this mechanism seriously, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appear to be finely tuned cancellations \nin order to get the masses reasonable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other takeaway is that the Standard Model \nfails to realize the dream of Einstein, Feynman  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531840,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Dirac. They, and perhaps we, want a singular \nultimate theory, one from which every property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the world should be derivable in a non-dippy, \nnon-arbitrary, and mathematically self-consistent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. The Standard Model isn’t that, and so we can \nimagine a more true, more general theory—perhaps a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of quantum gravity—of which the Standard \nModel, general relativity, and everything else  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are slightly crappy approximations, each \nwith only a limited range of validity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about this stuff last time—how we \nhave these layers of theory, with theories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describing larger scales and lower energies \nbeing “course-grainings” of deeper theories.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of those so-called effective theories \nare entirely defined by the theory at the bottom,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least that’s how the world works in the \nreductionist paradigm. We like to call the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more course-grained, more zoomed-out, more \nemergent theory the infrared or IR theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the deeper, more fundamental \ntheory is the ultraviolet or UV theory.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason is by analogy with the ultraviolet \ncatastrophe, which was in the last episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s say we have an IR theory that’s \na course-graining of a UV theory. Both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are defined by their own set of parameters. \nFor example, there are 19 free parameters in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Standard Model that seem arbitrary in \nthe context of that theory alone. The free  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parameters of the IR theory are not free in the \ncontext of the UV theory from which it comes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead they are “calculable” in principle \nfrom the parameters of the UV theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What are the parameters of the deepest UV theory? \nWe don’t know. Knowing that fully means knowing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Theory of Everything. But we can at least \nimagine that the UV theory of our universe lives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some sort of platonic space of all imaginable \nsuch theories. The UV theory that generates our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653680,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe has a specific location in that space \ndefined by its specific parameters. If we locate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that specific UV theory and you should be able to \ncalculate the precise parameters of the IR theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that inevitably emerges from it. At least if \nEinstein has his way. Einstein would also like the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UV theory itself to be inevitable, but really we \nhave no idea how those parameters were “chosen”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, how does naturalness play into this picture? \nImagine making a new universe by choosing a random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689440,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spot in theory-space. The resulting UV theory \ndefines the IR parameters like the Higgs mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. You haven’t chosen your IR theory with \nany intention to get specific IR parameters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you probably wouldn’t expect them to \nbe unusual or interesting in any way. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708000,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are, it would seem unnatural \n… not as random as you imagined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try an analogy. You shoot an arrow into \na barn wall. Blindfolded. You look to see where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you hit—just some random patch of wood. Do you \nact all surprised, like, “What are the chances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that I hit this square inch in particular? \nHow lucky!” No, you had to hit somewhere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s where you hit. Perfectly natural. On \nthe other hand, imagine if there was a target  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "painted on the wall with a very tiny bullseye. You \nhit the bullseye by chance without even knowing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743920,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was there. You’d be justified in thinking it a \ncrazy fluke, and not “just some spot on the wall”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In case you missed it, in this analogy the \nbarn wall is the space of possible UV theories.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each spot on the wall is a different set of \nparameters for that theory—each a different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underlying master theory leading to a \ndifferent universe with different IR  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parameters. The bullseye is a very particular \nUV theory, and it's special because it happens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to lead to a very special IR theory … in our \ncase a small Higgs mass and small cosmological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778960,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant. And the blind archer? God, perhaps? Or \nperhaps we could think of it as representing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanism by which the parameters of the \nUV theory are set. This could be random,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning the whole barn wall is fair game. Or those \nparameters could be set by some mechanism that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intrinsic to the UV theory—a mechanism that only \npermits a single outcome—only one spot on the barn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wall was ever possible in the first place. And \nonce you know the UV theory, you see that no other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set of UV parameters are possible. That seems to \nbe what Einstein preferred. God has no choice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818640,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But whether the UV parameters are set randomly \nor are unique and inevitable, the archer is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still blind and we do still have the problem of \nfine tuning. How so? Well, the blindness of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "archer doesn’t really represent the freedom of the \nUV theory to have different parameters. What it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really represents is a combination of two things: \n1) Our own lack of knowledge of those parameters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know nothing about them, and so they could be \nanything—the whole barn wall is open as far as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our prior knowledge is concerned. And 2) the fact \nthat the UV theory is blind to the IR theory. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if the mechanisms that set the parameters of \nthe UV theory have no choice in where they land,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they aren’t doing that with the emergent IR theory \nin mind. At least, that’s the default assumption  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of reductionism. So that’s the situation we’re \nin—we find an arrow in the bullseye, and it seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the arrow was put in place by a process \nthat had no idea that the bullseye existed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can talk about all of this in terms of Bayesian \nreasoning—by assessing likelihoods relative to our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prior knowledge. When physicists talk about \na “prior distribution” for the parameter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space of the UV theory, they typically aren’t \ntalking about some real process that sets the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parameters of that theory. Instead, they \nusually mean a probability distribution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that quantifies our degree of knowledge and \nignorance about some aspect of the world.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907520,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the possible range for the parameters given \nwhat little we know what we call a Bayesian prior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality the true parameters of \nthe UV theory are what they are,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "presumably a set of well defined values. But \nwith little prior knowledge, their possible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution with respect to that knowledge \nis vast. In our Bayesian reasoning we’d use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a wide Bayesian prior because its job is \nexclusively to represent our knowledge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now let's say we know that this wide-open UV \ntheory-space maps to some as-yet unmeasured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared parameter. We go to measure the \nparameter and ask “how much of the theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space in our Bayesian prior could produce an \nIR parameter like the one we just measured?”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the answer is a vanishingly small fraction, \nthen we might be suspicious. That’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case with the Higgs mass and cosmological \nconstant. It’s the arrow being suspiciously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the bullseye. We would be justified in \nassuming that the archer actually peeked,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or that there’s some powerful collusion in the \nUV theory that propagates down to the IR theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this case working to make the IR \ntheory the low-energy theory that it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it doesn’t really matter if the mechanism \nfor suppressing IR parameters such as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs mass is quantum cancellations \nor anything else. As long as our UV  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory natively lives at much higher energy \nthan the IR theory, either collusion between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000560,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the high-energy mechanics or extreme chance is \nneeded to yield that stable low energy theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008800,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s why it is reasonable to wonder \nat things like the smallness of the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass and cosmological constant. Not because \nthey’re actually unnatural, but because they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem unnatural given the mechanisms we think are \nat work behind them. Nature is obviously being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfectly natural, and so it means we’re missing \nsomething. Either the archer peeked—that’s the UV  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory being influenced by the IR theory—it \nsees the target. Or the archer shot many,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many arrows and the whole barn wall is \na pin-cushion of alternate universes and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of course we’re in one of the extremely rare \nbullseye universes with Survivable higgs mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046400,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. I keep saying we’ll come back to these \nideas of UV-IR mixing and anthropic reasoning,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054160,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we still will. But the choice between \nthese two may be our answer to Einstein’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question. Did God have a choice in creating the \nworld? If no then there was only one arrow and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that seems to demand a connection between the \nfundamental and the emergent that we don’t fully  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076080,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand. If yes then the arrow could have \nlanded anywhere on that wall and perhaps did,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solving naturalness but generating a multiverse. \nOf course, in that case we can just redefine the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiverse as the world and say that it is\nsingular and inevitable and Einstein gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be right again. Just like he was with \nall that stuff about curved spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102320,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting \nPBS. Anydesk was created because,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether you’re in a remote location or just \nworking from home, the need for reliable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "secure communication and data transfer remains \nimportant. With Anydesk, secure, direct file  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transfer bypasses the cloud entirely, allowing \nyou to move large media files, benchmarks, logs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or firmware updates between systems. And \nif you need to run complex systems, Anydesk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offers remote server monitoring \u0026 CMS access that \nallows you to control dashboards and monitor web  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "servers directly from your phone or tablet. Or \nif you need to set up remote 3D printing access,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can combine Anydesk with computer modules \nto run, monitor and print farm from just about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1151040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anywhere in the world. Anydesk is available for \npersonal use, but if your research requires a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot of global coordination, Anydesk offers \ncustom-tailored business plans. To learn more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162800,
    "dDurationMs": 18160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about Anydesk, go to anydesk.com/spacetime \nor check out the link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
